ally he said  that he supported the call for a police  inquiry  um do you think that the police should  look into mr hancock's  uh behavior  sorry i couldn't hear that very well  could you just say that again there's  something about  this of course yeah of course i'm trying  to work out how far labour wants to push  this  when neil ferguson uh ran into  trouble uh mr hancock supported a call  for a police inquiry and i'm just  wondering whether labour  would do the same in this situation  well my colleague fleur anderson has  referred this to  the police because she wants to know we  want to know  whether the police will be looking at  whether matt hancock did  break the law in um in his actions  at the time you'll remember when this  video was was taken we were all told  that we could only have close contact  with those that we were in a bubble with  they were our bubble  and that was the only people we could  have close contact with that was the law  at the time  yet it now turns out that matt hancock  was actually in two bubbles at the same  time  unbeknown to other people in that bubble  and that is how  infection spreads so yes there are  serious issues here which need further  investigation and we will be  uh looking at those because of course  you know matt hancock was the first to  say that  neil ferguson and others should have  resigned when they broke the rules and  that other people  needed to abide by them so you can't  have the rule maker  also being the rule breaker people want  to know there's accountability in that  the government obviously wants to limit  this to an issue of one individual's  conduct  but i think from what labour leaders  have been saying  you think that this is endemic in  government and i just want to  pick up on a couple of the points that  you've made you've said that um  mrs collidangelo is an old university  friend of  mr hancock's appointed as a  non-executive  at uh the department of health um  but of course that the point 